Q: How do I add comments to package.json for npm install? A: <p>Another practical option is to keep <code>package.json</code> as the real/canonical manifest, and maintain a companion <code>package.json5</code> file for human-readable documentation.</p>
<pre><code>`package.json` itself still has to be strict JSON, so comments like this are invalid:

```json
{
  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
    // why this dependency exists
    &quot;some-package&quot;: &quot;^1.0.0&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But you can keep the comments in <code>package.json5</code> instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>{
  // Package metadata used by npm.
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;my-project&quot;,

  // Development and release scripts.
  &quot;scripts&quot;: {
    // Compile TypeScript.
    &quot;build&quot;: &quot;tsc -p tsconfig.json&quot;,
  },

  // Runtime dependencies.
  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
    // Used for ...
    &quot;some-package&quot;: &quot;^1.0.0&quot;,
  },
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then sync the documented file from the real manifest so the data does not drift.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;scripts&quot;: {
    &quot;sync:json5&quot;: &quot;json5-manifest-sync&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Run:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>npm run sync:json5
</code></pre>
<p>That keeps <code>package.json</code> as the file consumed by npm/yarn/pnpm, while <code>package.json5</code> acts as the commented documentation version.</p>
<p>This avoids several of the common hacks mentioned in this thread:</p>
<ul>
<li>duplicate keys, which can be removed or normalized by tooling</li>
<li><code>&quot;//&quot;</code> pseudo-fields that only work in limited places</li>
<li>separate <code>dependenciesComments</code> / <code>scriptsComments</code> sections that drift away from the thing they document</li>
</ul>
<p>Disclosure: I wrote <a href="https://github.com/BBaysinger/json5-manifest-sync" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>json5-manifest-sync</code></a>, which implements this approach. The general idea does not require that specific package: keep strict <code>package.json</code> canonical, keep comments in a companion JSON5 file, and sync from the canonical manifest rather than asking npm to read JSON5 directly.</p>
